#N canvas 20 20 2000 1400 12;
#X text 20 20 m_nanokey -- the Korg nanoKEY's input layer: its keys \, from any channel of its port \, out a cord as <note> <velocity> \, 0 for a release. It knows nothing about which synth a key plays: c_synth decides that. ref/device/nanokey.md, f 70;
#X text 20 150 creation arg 1: the first Pd channel of the nanoKEY's port \, 49 on the device: Midi-In 4 \, which it shares with the Volca interface's DIN IN \, where nothing arrives. inlets: none. outlet 1: <note> <velocity> \, to c_synth., f 70;
#X obj 20 400 notein;
#X obj 400 470 - \$1;
#X obj 400 520 t f f;
#X obj 400 570 >= 0;
#X obj 560 570 < 16;
#X obj 400 620 *;
#X obj 20 560 pack f f;
#X obj 20 640 spigot;
#X obj 20 720 outlet;
#X text 700 400 notein fires right to left: the channel opens or shuts the spigot \, the velocity lands in pack's cold inlet \, then the note sends the pair. Any channel of the port \, 0 <= channel - block < 16 \, so a nanoKEY set to another channel still plays \, and the Launchpad \, the nano and the Streichfett \, on the ports below \, never do., f 50;
#X text 700 700 Not polled \, and not on the presence roster: it would be the sixth row of g_oled's diag layer \, which is bounded at five \, so it registers nothing \, not even none. The OLED's revision will lift the bound. Its identity reply carries Korg's 42 \, the nanoKONTROL's byte \, so a poll would also be read as the nano by c_devid 66 (ref/device/nanokey.md)., f 50;
#X obj 20 1000 loadbang;
#X obj 20 1050 del 2000;
#X obj 20 1100 f \$1;
#X obj 20 1150 print m_nanokey-channel;
#X text 300 1050 Prints the channel block it got \, since a wrong count of creation arguments loads in silence. C-9, f 50;
#X connect 2 2 3 0;
#X connect 3 0 4 0;
#X connect 4 1 6 0;
#X connect 6 0 7 1;
#X connect 4 0 5 0;
#X connect 5 0 7 0;
#X connect 7 0 9 1;
#X connect 2 1 8 1;
#X connect 2 0 8 0;
#X connect 8 0 9 0;
#X connect 9 0 10 0;
#X connect 13 0 14 0;
#X connect 14 0 15 0;
#X connect 15 0 16 0;
